      "user": "make flashcards of this content.",
      "nexa": "Here are the flashcards:\n\nQ: What is the purpose of normalization in database design?\nA: Normalization helps eliminate redundant data and improve data integrity by organizing data into separate tables.\n\nQ: What problem does redundant data pose to a relational database?\nA: Redundant data can lead to inconsistencies, errors, and difficulties in maintaining data accuracy.\n\nQ: What describes the relationship between attributes in a relation?\nA: Functional dependency is a concept that explains how one attribute determines another attribute's value.\n\nQ: How do you identify functional dependencies for a given relation?\nA: You analyze the relationships between attributes by asking if changing an attribute's value would affect other attributes' values.\n\nQ: What process does normalization use to group attributes into relations in known normal form?\nA: Normalization uses functional dependencies to reorganize data and create tables that meet specific normal forms (e.g., 1NF, 2NF, 3NF).\n\nQ: What are the most commonly used normal forms in database design?\nA: First Normal Form (1NF), Second Normal Form (2NF), and Third Normal Form (3NF) are the primary normal forms.\n\nQ: What problems arise when a relation breaks the rules of 1NF or 2NF?\nA: Breaking"
    },
